<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Greater Poland</h1><p class="st-meta">1138 CE – 1320 CE (approx.) · capital Poznan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The duchy of the Piast heartland around Poznan and Gniezno, which produced Poland's first crowned king after two centuries of division.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Under the testament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Boleslaw III</a> in 1138, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-iii-the-old">Mieszko III the Old</a> received Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>, the cradle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast</a> state. For much of the next century the duchy was contested between Mieszko's descendants and was at times divided between Poznan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was a farming region, settled in the 13th century by German colonists and towns founded under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> law, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznan</a> in 1253. Its dukes, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-odonic">Wladyslaw Odonic</a> and his sons, built up their power with the support of the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii">Przemysl II</a> gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a> in 1294 and was crowned King of Poland in Gniezno in 1295.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii">Przemysl II</a> was murdered in 1296, and the duchy passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-i-lokietek">Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-glogow">Henry of Glogow</a>. Wladyslaw I took it back in 1314, and in 1320 it became part of the reunited <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1025-1385">Kingdom of Poland</a> (1025-1385).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-greater-poland.jpg" alt="Duchy of Greater Poland" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: Zuber &amp;amp; MariuszR · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polska_za_W_Lokietka_(1275-1300).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1138 CE – 1320 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Poznan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mieszko III the Old, Wladyslaw Odonic, Przemysl II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Greater Poland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1229&amp;focus=duchy-of-greater-poland" aria-label="Duchy of Greater Poland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5060 369 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Greater Poland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5380 625L5457 692"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 625h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 625h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Poznan. <a href="/atlas?year=1229&amp;focus=duchy-of-greater-poland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
